## ⚠ CAUTION: never run compute on the login node

The login node is shared with every other cluster user.

- Allowed on the login node: `sbatch`, `squeue`, `sacct`, `rsync`, `git`, `tmux`,
  `module avail`, and light file operations.
- Not allowed on the login node: a training entrypoint, model loading, CUDA
  operations, and heavy compilation.

For an interactive GPU session, run `srun --pty --gres=gpu:1 -p gpu bash`. Run it
inside tmux, so an SSH drop does not kill it.

## Run experiments through unityHPC/run.sh, never with raw sbatch

```bash
set -a && . unityHPC/.env && set +a
bash unityHPC/run.sh run <exp>          # submit one job and record it
bash unityHPC/run.sh status             # live job counts from sacct
bash unityHPC/run.sh fetch              # rsync _results/ back
bash unityHPC/run.sh logs               # tail the newest job's output
```

`run.sh run` records every job in `.unity_state/<campaign>.db`. A job submitted
with bare `sbatch` is invisible to every management tool.

For a batch of related jobs, run `/unity-hpc:sweep`. It gives them one campaign.

## Local checks, with no SSH

```bash
hpc-status       # reads .unity_state/*.db — exit 0=done 1=running 2=failed
hpc-results      # print results.tsv with color
hpc-usage --last 7d --plot util   # terminal plot of cluster GPU usage
hpc-tmux ls      # list the tmux sessions on the cluster
```

## Pick a backend

Run `/unity-hpc:recommend-hardware`. It ranks GPU types by your measured speed
against current availability. For the raw numbers, call
`mcp__unity-hpc__unity_usage` or `mcp__unity-hpc__unity_traffic`.

## tmux sessions on the cluster

A tmux session survives an SSH drop.

- `unity-collector` — the cluster-intel telemetry collector, one 15-minute sample
  loop
- `interactive` — for `srun --pty` GPU sessions

Manage them with `hpc-tmux ls | start <name> [cmd] | attach <name> | kill <name>`.

## Multi-node experiments

Set `SLURM_NODES=2` or more in `unityHPC/.env`. The `ml` job template then runs the
job under `torchrun`.

Before a sweep, read `MaxJobs` from the shared cluster configuration. Call
`mcp__unity-hpc__unity_cluster_profile` to get it.

## Constraints **(project-specific — replace these)**

- Do NOT modify `prepare.py`. It is the read-only evaluation harness.
- Do NOT install a package that `pyproject.toml` does not list.
- Only `train.py` is open to edits. Everything inside it is fair game.
- VRAM is a soft constraint. A meaningful gain justifies a modest increase.

## Results

- `_results/<campaign>/` — gitignored. It lives on `/work` on the cluster, and
  rsyncs back to here.
- `results.tsv` — tab-separated. Put no tab inside a field.
- Campaign names: `auto_YYYY-MM-DD`, or a descriptive tag.
- Log every experiment with `/unity-hpc:log-result`.

## Resuming after a context reset

Run `/unity-hpc:resume`. It reads `results.tsv`, the campaign ledger, the shared
cluster configuration and the current queue state. Do not re-read every file by
hand.

## Fair use

- Per-user SLURM limits apply under the account `pi_yourlab_umass_edu`. Read them
  with `mcp__unity-hpc__unity_cluster_profile`.
- Unity is shared with other users. Before a large sweep, check the queue depth
  with `mcp__unity-hpc__unity_traffic`.
- The cluster-intel collector is opt-in. If this project enabled it, it samples
  the cluster every 15 minutes from a tmux session on the login node.
